    # Section 1: Background and Rationale
    doc.append('<details class="med-details"><summary>')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('## 1. Background and Rationale</summary><div class="details-content">')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('Intraoperative transesophageal echocardiography (TEE) has evolved from an investigational tool into an essential clinical component of modern cardiac surgery. Multiple societal practice guidelines (ASE, SCA, ASA, ACC, AHA, STS) assign Class I recommendations for routine TEE in all adult open cardiac and thoracic aortic procedures, and in catheter-based structural heart interventions.')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('The operating room environment presents unique diagnostic conditions that distinguish intraoperative TEE from outpatient examinations:')
    doc.append('- General anesthesia, mechanical positive-pressure ventilation, neuromuscular blockade, and altered loading conditions (hypovolemia, vasodilation, myocardial stunning) profoundly impact cardiac chamber dimensions and Doppler hemodynamics.')
    doc.append('- The echocardiographer must reconcile preoperative outpatient findings with the immediate intraoperative state, identifying dynamic shifts in valvular regurgitation, loading-dependent outflow gradients, and right ventricular functional reserve.')
    doc.append('- Surgical intervention alters cardiac geometry instantaneously. Post-CPB evaluation requires targeted interrogation for specific iatrogenic risks, paravalvular leaks, residual defects, myocardial ischemia, and adequate de-airing before chest closure.')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('This document provides a comprehensive, surgery-based roadmap detailing standardized preprocedure and postprocedure imaging strategies for each cardiovascular operative category.')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('</div></details>')
    doc.append('')

    # Section 2: General Principles & RV Function Assessment
    doc.append('<details class="med-details"><summary>')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('## 2. General Principles & Right Ventricular Function Assessment</summary><div class="details-content">')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### Baseline Standard Examination')
    doc.append('A comprehensive baseline TEE examination should precede cardiopulmonary bypass in every patient, systematically evaluating the left ventricle, right ventricle, left atrium, left atrial appendage, interatrial septum, all four cardiac valves, thoracic aorta, and pulmonary veins, unless contraindicated by severe esophageal pathology.')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### Assessment of Right Ventricular Function by TEE')
    doc.append('Right ventricular (RV) failure is a catastrophic cause of post-bypass morbidity and mortality across cardiac surgery, particularly in mitral valve surgery, heart transplantation, and mechanical circulatory support. Because the complex crescentic geometry of the RV precludes single-plane volumetric formulas, a multi-parametric quantitative assessment is mandatory.')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### Table 1: Echocardiographic Assessment of Right Ventricular Function Using Transesophageal Echocardiography')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Parameter | TEE View / Modality | Cutoff for Dysfunction | Advantages & Strengths | Limitations & Caveats in OR |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Fractional Area Change (FAC)** | ME 4-chamber view ($0^\circ	ext{--}20^\circ$) | **$< 35\%$** | Encompasses both longitudinal and radial inward motion of the free wall and septum; validated against cardiac MRI. | Requires clear endocardial border tracing; trabeculations must be carefully excluded from myocardial area. |')
    doc.append('| **TAPSE (M-mode)** | ME 4-chamber or modified deep transgastric | **$< 17\text{ mm}$** | Rapid, reproducible, quantitative metric of basal-apical longitudinal contraction. | Angle-dependent; overestimates global RV function in regional tethering or post-pericardiotomy states. |')
    doc.append('| **Tissue Doppler S\' Wave** | ME 4-chamber, pulsed-wave DTI at lateral tricuspid annulus | **$< 9.5\text{ cm/s}$** | Angle-corrected velocity of longitudinal myocardial displacement; independent of image quality. | Reflects solely base-to-apex motion; influenced by translational heart motion during open pericardium. |')
    doc.append('| **RV Free Wall Longitudinal Strain** | ME 4-chamber speckle-tracking | **$> -20\%$** (less negative) | Detects subclinical RV myocardial dysfunction before drop in ejection fraction or FAC; prognostic value. | Vendor-dependent; requires stable ECG gating, high frame rate ($> 50\text{ Hz}$), and clear endocardial tracking. |')
    doc.append('| **RV Fractional Area Change (Transgastric)** | TG mid-papillary SAX | Qualitative / semi-quantitative | Direct visualization of RV crescentic compression against the septum; ideal for monitoring CPB weaning. | Foreshortening risk; lacks standardized quantitative cutoff values. |')
    doc.append('| **Interventricular Septal Curvature** | TG mid-papillary SAX ($0^\circ$) | Leftward septal flattening (D-shaped LV) | Differentiates RV pressure overload (flattening in systole and diastole) vs volume overload (flattening in diastole only). | Loading dependent; altered by bundle branch blocks, epicardial pacing, or prior pericardiotomy. |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('</div></details>')
    doc.append('')

    # Section 3: Valve Surgery
    doc.append('<details class="med-details"><summary>')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('## 3. Valve Surgery</summary><div class="details-content">')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### 3.1. Mitral Valve Surgery')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('#### Preprocedure Assessment')
    doc.append('Pre-CPB evaluation of the mitral valve (MV) establishes the etiology (degenerative, functional, ischemic, rheumatic, endocarditis), mechanism according to Carpentier\'s classification (Type I: normal leaflet motion; Type II: leaflet prolapse/flail; Type IIIa: restricted in diastole/systole; Type IIIb: restricted in systole), and repairability.')
    doc.append('- **3D TEE "Surgical View":** Real-time 3D volume rendering from the left atrial perspective ("surgeon\'s view" with aorta at 12 o\'clock, left atrial appendage at 9 o\'clock) maps individual scallop pathology (A1, A2, A3; P1, P2, P3; anterolateral and posteromedial commissures).')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('<span id="fig-01"></span>')
    doc.append('![Figure 1: 3D surgical en face view of the mitral valve](images/fig01_3d_mitral_valve_en_face.jpg)')
    doc.append('*Figure 1: 3D surgical en face view of the mitral valve from the left atrial perspective. (A) Standard orientation with the aortic valve at 12 o\'clock, left atrial appendage at 9 o\'clock, showing anterior leaflet scallops (A1, A2, A3) and posterior leaflet scallops (P1, P2, P3). (B) Color flow Doppler mapping demonstrating eccentric regurgitant jet trajectory corresponding to P2 prolapse.*')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('#### Prediction of Post-Repair Systolic Anterior Motion (SAM)')
    doc.append('Systolic anterior motion (SAM) of the anterior mitral leaflet produces acute dynamic left ventricular outflow tract (LVOT) obstruction and severe recurrent MR after ring annuloplasty. Pre-CPB geometric risk factors must be measured in the ME LVOT / LAX view:')
    doc.append('1. **Ratio of Anterior to Posterior Leaflet Length ($AL/PL$):** Ratio $< 1.3$ indicates an elongated posterior leaflet that pushes the coaptation line anteriorly into the LVOT.')
    doc.append('2. **Coaptation-to-Septum Distance ($C\text{--}Sept$):** Distance $< 25\text{ mm}$ strongly predisposes to SAM.')
    doc.append('3. **Basal Septal Thickness:** Asymmetric septal bulge ($> 15\text{ mm}$) narrows the LVOT entry angle.')
    doc.append('4. **Aorto-Mitral Angle:** Acute angle ($< 120^\circ$) increases drag forces on the anterior leaflet.')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('<span id="fig-02"></span>')
    doc.append('![Figure 2: Echocardiographic measurements for predicting SAM](images/fig02_sam_risk_measurements.jpg)')
    doc.append('*Figure 2: Echocardiographic measurements for predicting the risk of systolic anterior motion (SAM) after mitral valve repair. ME long-axis view showing measurement of coaptation-to-septum distance (C-Sept), anterior leaflet length (AL), posterior leaflet length (PL), and basal interventricular septal thickness.*')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('#### Table 2: Key Points for Pre-Surgical Assessment in Mitral Valve Surgery')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Category | Specific TEE Checklist Items |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Etiology & Mechanism** | • Differentiate degenerative (fibroelastic deficiency vs Barlow disease) from functional/ischemic or rheumatic disease.<br>• Identify Carpentier classification (Type I, II, IIIa, IIIb) and identify prolapsed/flail scallops (A1-A3, P1-P3). |')
    doc.append('| **Quantitative Severity** | • Measure vena contracta width (VC $\ge 7\text{ mm}$ = severe), PISA radius, EROA ($\ge 0.40\text{ cm}^2$ primary; $\ge 0.20\text{ cm}^2$ secondary), regurgitant volume ($\ge 60\text{ mL}$).<br>• Pulmonary venous flow reversal in systole (ME 4Ch / RUPV view). |')
    doc.append('| **SAM Risk Factors** | • Coaptation-to-septum distance $< 25\text{ mm}$, $AL/PL\text{ ratio} < 1.3$, basal septal thickness $> 15\text{ mm}$, hyperdynamic small LV cavity. |')
    doc.append('| **Associated Lesions** | • Exclude left atrial / LAA thrombus (mandatory before manipulation).<br>• Quantify tricuspid regurgitation and measure tricuspid annular diameter ($\ge 40\text{ mm}$ or $> 21\text{ mm/m}^2$ indicates concomitant annuloplasty).<br>• Assess aortic valve for occult AR (may worsen or hinder cardioplegia delivery). |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('#### Minimally Invasive and Robotic Mitral Surgery')
    doc.append('Minimally invasive and robotic approaches require unique TEE guidance:')
    doc.append('- **Venous Cannulation:** Transesophageal guidance of bicaval or femoral venous cannulas into the SVC-RA and IVC-RA junctions.')
    doc.append('- **Endoaortic Balloon (Endoclamp):** Real-time tracking of the endoaortic balloon catheter advanced from the femoral artery into the ascending aorta ($2	ext{--}3\text{ cm}$ above the sinotubular junction and proximal to the brachiocephalic takeoff). Color Doppler verifies complete aortic occlusion and absence of balloon migration or dissection.')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('<span id="fig-03"></span>')
    doc.append('![Figure 3: Intraoperative TEE monitoring of endoaortic balloon](images/fig03_me_aortic_valve_endoballoon.jpg)')
    doc.append('*Figure 3: Intraoperative TEE monitoring of endoaortic balloon in robotic/minimally invasive mitral surgery. (A) ME aortic valve LAX view showing inflated endoballoon positioned in the ascending aorta above the sinotubular junction. (B) Color Doppler confirms complete seal and absence of cardioplegia malperfusion or balloon slippage into the coronary ostia.*')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('#### Table 3: Key Points for Post-Surgical Assessment in Mitral Valve Surgery')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Category | Specific TEE Checklist Items |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Adequacy of Repair** | • Confirm coaptation length $\ge 5	ext{--}8\text{ mm}$ in ME 4Ch and LAX views.<br>• Exclude residual MR: any residual MR greater than mild ($> 1+$) warrants immediate surgeon notification.<br>• Rule out iatrogenic mitral stenosis: transmitral mean gradient must be $< 5\text{ mmHg}$ at physiological heart rates; effective orifice area $> 1.5	ext{--}1.8\text{ cm}^2$. |')
    doc.append('| **SAM & LVOT Obstruction** | • Inspect anterior leaflet for dynamic systolic anterior motion; measure peak LVOT gradient by CWD (resting gradient $> 30\text{ mmHg}$ or provocative $> 50\text{ mmHg}$ indicates significant obstruction).<br>• Manage medically first (volume expansion, beta-blockade, cessation of inotropes, phenylephrine); persistent severe SAM with refractory shock warrants surgical revision. |')
    doc.append('| **Circumflex Artery Injury** | • The circumflex artery courses adjacent to the lateral mitral annulus (P1/A1). Circumflex distortion or suture entrapment causes acute lateral/inferolateral wall motion abnormalities on transgastric SAX. |')
    doc.append('| **Prosthetic Valve Seating** | • In MV replacement: verify normal leaflet opening/closing arcs; exclude leaflet impingement by preserved subvalvular apparatus; quantify transvalvular vs paravalvular leak (PVL). |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('---')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### 3.2. Aortic Valve Surgery')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('#### Preprocedure Assessment')
    doc.append('Aortic valve evaluation requires precise anatomical measurements in the ME aortic valve LAX view ($110^\circ	ext{--}130^\circ$) at end-diastole (or mid-systole for annulus):')
    doc.append('1. **Aortic Annulus (hinge-to-hinge diameter in mid-systole)**')
    doc.append('2. **Sinuses of Valsalva diameter**')
    doc.append('3. **Sinotubular Junction (STJ) diameter**')
    doc.append('4. **Ascending Aorta diameter ($2	ext{--}3\text{ cm}$ above STJ)**')
    doc.append('5. **Distance from Annulus to Coronary Ostia (left and right coronary arteries)**')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('<span id="fig-04"></span>')
    doc.append('![Figure 4: Echocardiographic measurements of the aortic root](images/fig04_aortic_root_measurements.jpg)')
    doc.append('*Figure 4: Echocardiographic measurements of the aortic root in ME long-axis view. Standard dimensions include: (1) aortic annulus, (2) sinuses of Valsalva, (3) sinotubular junction (STJ), (4) proximal tubular ascending aorta, and (5) coronary ostial height.*')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('#### Table 4: Key Points for Pre-Surgical Assessment in Aortic Valve Surgery')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Category | Specific TEE Checklist Items |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Aortic Stenosis (AS)** | • Measure peak velocity, mean transvalvular gradient, and continuity equation aortic valve area (AVA).<br>• Confirm flow state: normal flow high gradient vs low-flow low-gradient (classical low-EF vs paradoxical normal-EF).<br>• Assess basal septal hypertrophy (risk of post-AVR subaortic stenosis or "suicide LV"). |')
    doc.append('| **Aortic Regurgitation (AR)**| • Classify mechanism (Type 1: root/STJ dilation; Type 2: prolapse/fenestration; Type 3: retraction/calcification).<br>• Measure vena contracta ($\ge 6\text{ mm}$), PHT ($< 200\text{ ms}$), holodiastolic flow reversal in descending thoracic aorta (ME descending aorta view).<br>• Evaluate feasibility of aortic valve repair vs valve-sparing root replacement (David vs Yacoub procedures). |')
    doc.append('| **Bicuspid Aortic Valve** | • Classify morphotype (Sievers classification: Type 0, 1, 2; raphe presence and orientation).<br>• Screen for associated aortopathy (ascending aneurysm) and coarctation of the aorta. |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('#### Table 5: Key Points for Post-Surgical Assessment in Aortic Valve Surgery')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Category | Specific TEE Checklist Items |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Prosthetic Valve Seating** | • Evaluate mechanical disk excursion or bioprosthetic leaflet mobility.<br>• Measure peak velocity, mean gradient, and Doppler Velocity Index ($DVI = VTI_{LVOT} / VTI_{AV}$; normal $> 0.30$; $< 0.25$ suggests obstruction or patient-prosthesis mismatch [PPM]). |')
    doc.append('| **Paravalvular Leak (PVL)** | • Interrogate the entire circumference in ME AV SAX ($30^\circ	ext{--}60^\circ$) and LAX views.<br>• Distinguish transvalvular washing jets (normal) from paravalvular regurgitant jets (pathological). Any moderate/severe PVL requires consideration of immediate re-CPB and re-suturing. |')
    doc.append('| **Coronary Perfusion & Ostia**| • Suture entrapment or ostial kinking of left main or right coronary artery causes acute regional wall motion abnormalities.<br>• Continuously monitor anterior (LAD) and inferior (RCA) wall kinetics on transgastric mid-papillary SAX. |')
    doc.append('| **Aortotomy & Root Integrity**| • Screen for aortic dissection flap originating from aortotomy closure or cross-clamp site; exclude aortic root pseudoaneurysm. |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('---')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### 3.3. Tricuspid and Pulmonary Valve Surgery')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('#### Table 6: Key Points for Tricuspid Valve Assessment')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Phase | Essential Echocardiographic Checklist |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Preprocedure** | • Differentiate functional/secondary TR (annular dilation, RV remodeling) from primary/organic TR (endocarditis, carcinoid, pacemaker lead-induced).<br>• Measure end-diastolic tricuspid annular diameter in ME 4Ch ($> 40\text{ mm}$ or $> 21\text{ mm/m}^2$ warrants concomitant annuloplasty even if TR is mild/moderate).<br>• Quantify TR: vena contracta $\ge 7\text{ mm}$, hepatic vein systolic flow reversal, dense triangular CWD jet. |')
    doc.append('| **Postprocedure** | • Assess residual TR (goal $\le$ mild); confirm absence of tricuspid stenosis (mean gradient $< 3	ext{--}5\text{ mmHg}$).<br>• Screen for conduction injury: AV node and bundle of His lie at the apex of the triangle of Koch; suture injury causes complete AV block.<br>• Check right coronary artery (RCA) in the AV groove: annular ring sutures can cinch or occlude the RCA, producing acute RV free wall ischemia and acute RV failure. |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('#### Table 7: Key Points for Pulmonic Valve Assessment')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Phase | Essential Echocardiographic Checklist |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Preprocedure** | • Evaluated in ME RV inflow-outflow ($50^\circ	ext{--}70^\circ$) and upper esophageal (UE) aortic arch views.<br>• Quantify PS (peak velocity, mean gradient) and PR (jet width, pressure half-time, branch PA diastolic reversal).<br>• Assess RVOT conduit pathology in adult congenital reoperations. |')
    doc.append('| **Postprocedure** | • Verify prosthetic pulmonary valve or homograft seating; measure transvalvular gradients; exclude paravalvular regurgitation and RVOT obstruction. |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('</div></details>')
    doc.append('')

    # Section 4: Coronary Artery Disease & CABG
    doc.append('<details class="med-details"><summary>')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('## 4. Coronary Artery Disease & CABG Surgery</summary><div class="details-content">')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### Table 8: Key Points for Pre- and Postprocedure Assessment in Coronary Artery Bypass Grafting (CABG)')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Procedure Stage | Echocardiographic Objectives & Clinical Decisions |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Pre-CPB Baseline** | • **17-Segment Regional Wall Motion:** Map baseline wall motion score index (normokinetic, hypokinetic, akinetic, dyskinetic, aneurysmal) across LAD, LCx, and RCA territories.<br>• **Aortic Atheroma Screening:** Epiaortic scanning and ME TEE imaging of the ascending aorta and arch to identify calcified, mobile, or ulcerated plaques (Grade IV/V), guiding cross-clamp placement or prompting "no-touch" off-pump techniques.<br>• **Occult Mitral Regurgitation:** Ischemic MR is dynamic and preload-dependent; general anesthesia may underestimate MR severity. |')
    doc.append('| **Post-CPB Weaning** | • **New Regional Wall Motion Abnormalities (RWMA):** Appearance of new hypokinesis or akinesis in a revascularized territory signifies graft thrombosis, spasm, kinking, or air embolus into a graft.<br>• **Weaning Hemodynamics:** Guide titration of inotropes, vasopressors, and volume based on biventricular filling, stroke volume, and contractility.<br>• **De-Airing:** Verify thorough venting of intracardiac air before release of cross-clamp to prevent coronary and cerebral air embolism. |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('</div></details>')
    doc.append('')

