    doc.append('| Pathology / Stage | Critical TEE Interrogation Points |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Aortic Atheroma Grading** | • Grade I: Normal to mild intimal thickening ($< 2\text{ mm}$).<br>• Grade II: Severe intimal thickening ($2	ext{--}3\text{ mm}$) without protrusion.<br>• Grade III: Protruding atheroma ($> 3\text{ mm}$) without mobility.<br>• Grade IV: Mobile atheroma protruding into lumen ($> 4\text{ mm}$).<br>• Grade V: Ulcerated, mobile, or calcified atheroma (extreme embolic risk). |')
    doc.append('| **Type A Aortic Dissection** | • Confirm intimal flap in ascending aorta, root, and arch; differentiate true lumen (expands in systole, systolic forward flow) from false lumen (expands in diastole, sluggish flow or thrombus).<br>• Identify entry tears and extension into coronary ostia (most commonly right coronary artery).<br>• Mechanism of acute AR: root dilation, leaflet flail/disruption, or prolapse of dissection flap through the valve.<br>• Exclude hemopericardium and cardiac tamponade. |')
    doc.append('| **Thoracic Aortic Aneurysm** | • Measure maximum orthogonal dimensions of root, STJ, ascending aorta, arch, and descending aorta; guide extent of surgical graft replacement (hemiarch vs total arch). |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### Table 10: Key Points for Postprocedure Assessment in Aortic Surgery')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Postprocedure Focus | Specific TEE Checklist Items |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Graft & Anastomoses** | • Interrogate proximal (coronary button reimplantation, root) and distal aortic anastomoses for suture line disruption, pseudoaneurysm, or severe bleeding.<br>• Confirm laminar flow through composite graft without kinking or narrowing. |')
    doc.append('| **True vs False Lumen Perfusion**| • In total arch / elephant trunk repairs, verify that the distal graft perfuses the true lumen and that visceral/iliac perfusion is intact without malperfusion syndrome. |')
    doc.append('| **De-Airing** | • Intensive continuous de-airing monitoring of aortic root, LV apex, and pulmonary veins before clamp release. |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('</div></details>')
    doc.append('')

    # Section 6: Mechanical Circulatory Support (MCS)
    doc.append('<details class="med-details"><summary>')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('## 6. Mechanical Circulatory Support (MCS)</summary><div class="details-content">')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### 6.1. Left Ventricular Assist Device (LVAD)')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('#### Pre-LVAD Echocardiographic Strategy')
    doc.append('A comprehensive pre-implantation scan must rule out or quantify five critical structural hazards:')
    doc.append('1. **Aortic Regurgitation (AR):** Any degree of AR $> \text{trace/mild}$ will recirculate in a closed loop from the outflow graft back through the aortic valve into the LVAD, dissipating systemic forward cardiac output. Concomitant aortic valve repair or closure (Park\'s stitch, central coaptation suture, or bioprosthetic replacement) is required.')
    doc.append('2. **Patent Foramen Ovale / ASD:** Left ventricular decompression lowers left atrial pressure below right atrial pressure, precipitating massive right-to-left shunting, profound refractory hypoxemia, and paradoxical embolism. PFO/ASD must be closed surgically at implant.')
    doc.append('3. **Right Ventricular Dysfunction:** Post-LVAD right heart failure occurs in $20	ext{--}40\%$ of patients due to acute septal shifting and increased venous return. Quantify RV FAC, TAPSE, free wall strain, and TR severity.')
    doc.append('4. **Aortic Valve Prosthesis:** Mechanical aortic valves must be removed or oversewn to prevent leaflet thrombosis.')
    doc.append('5. **LV Apical Thrombus:** Must be evacuated before inflow cannula insertion to prevent immediate pump thrombosis or systemic embolization.')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('<span id="fig-05"></span>')
    doc.append('![Figure 5: Pre-LVAD systematic imaging strategy](images/fig05_pre_lvad_imaging_strategy.jpg)')
    doc.append('*Figure 5: Proposed systematic imaging strategy starting from the ME 4-chamber view prior to LVAD implantation. Stepwise assessment of: (1) PFO/ASD, (2) RV size and function, (3) tricuspid regurgitation, (4) aortic regurgitation, and (5) LV apical thrombus.*')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('#### Post-LVAD Echocardiographic Strategy')
    doc.append('During and immediately after weaning from CPB, TEE evaluates:')
    doc.append('- **De-Airing:** Intensive scanning of LV cavity and root during initial pump speed ramp.')
    doc.append('- **Inflow Cannula Alignment:** Inflow cannula must be directed strictly along the long axis of the LV cavity towards the center of the mitral valve orifice, avoiding contact with the interventricular septum or lateral wall.')
    doc.append('- **Interventricular Septal Position:** Neutral, slightly bowing septal curvature indicates optimal pump speed. Leftward septal shift (suction event) indicates over-pumping, hypovolemia, or severe RV failure. Rightward septal shift indicates pump under-speed or obstruction.')
    doc.append('- **Outflow Graft Anastomosis:** Interrogate ascending aortic anastomosis by CFD and PWD for kinking, stenosis ($V_{max} < 2\text{ m/s}$), or dissection.')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('<span id="fig-06"></span>')
    doc.append('![Figure 6: LVAD inflow cannula alignment and Doppler interrogation](images/fig06_lvad_inflow_cannula_assessment.jpg)')
    doc.append('*Figure 6: Unobstructed LVAD inflow cannula. (A) ME 4-chamber view showing coaxial alignment of the inflow cannula within the LV apex directed towards the mitral valve. (B) 3D reconstruction confirming free space around cannula tip without tissue or septal obstruction.*')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('<span id="fig-07"></span>')
    doc.append('![Figure 7: Post-LVAD systematic imaging strategy](images/fig07_post_lvad_imaging_strategy.jpg)')
    doc.append('*Figure 7: Systematic imaging strategy starting from the ME 4-chamber view for assessment post-LVAD implantation. Evaluation of septal curvature, RV contractility, inflow velocity profiles, de-airing, and absence of aortic valve opening every few cycles.*')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('#### Table 11: Key Points for Pre- and Postprocedure Assessment for LVAD Implantation')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Phase | Essential Echocardiographic Checklist |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Pre-LVAD** | • Quantify AR (must be $\le$ mild); screen for PFO by agitated saline contrast with release of Valsalva.<br>• Measure RV FAC ($< 25\%$ high RHF risk), TAPSE, and TR severity.<br>• Exclude LV apical thrombus and mobile atheroma in ascending aorta at outflow graft site. |')
    doc.append('| **Post-LVAD** | • Verify inflow cannula position: centered in LV cavity, parallel to septum, free of trabeculae.<br>• Evaluate inflow Doppler spectrum: continuous forward flow ($1.0	ext{--}1.8\text{ m/s}$) with modest respiratory/cardiac modulation.<br>• Confirm neutral IVS position; optimize pump RPM to achieve intermittent aortic valve opening (every 2-3 beats).<br>• Interrogate outflow graft anastomosis for stenosis, kinking, or perivascular hematoma. |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('---')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### 6.2. Percutaneous Devices & Right Ventricular Assist Devices (RVAD)')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('#### Table 12: Key Points for Percutaneous Axial LVAD Placement (Impella)')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Assessment Phase | Critical Imaging Checklist |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Pre-Placement** | • Exclude aortic valve stenosis, mechanical prosthetic valve, and severe AR.<br>• Rule out LV apical thrombus and severe peripheral/axillary vascular disease. |')
    doc.append('| **Device Guidance** | • Real-time TEE guidance during trans-aortic valve crossing in ME AV LAX view ($110^\circ	ext{--}130^\circ$).<br>• Confirm inlet area sits precisely **$4.0	ext{--}4.5\text{ cm}$ below the aortic valve plane** in the mid-LV cavity, angled toward the apex and away from the papillary muscles and mitral apparatus.<br>• Confirm outlet area resides in the ascending aorta above the valve leaflets. |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('#### Table 13: Key Points for Right Ventricular Assist Device (RVAD) Placement')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Modality / Stage | Echocardiographic Objectives & Positioning |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Surgical RVAD** | • Inflow cannula: positioned in RA or RV free wall; confirm no suction against atrial septum.<br>• Outflow cannula: anastomosed to main pulmonary artery; exclude PA graft kinking. |')
    doc.append('| **Percutaneous RVAD (Impella RP, ProtekDuo)** | • TEE guidance of inflow basket in IVC-RA junction and outflow cannula across tricuspid and pulmonary valves into the main PA.<br>• Confirm no tricuspid leaflet entrapment or pulmonary valve disruption. |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('<span id="fig-08"></span>')
    doc.append('![Figure 8: Percutaneous RVAD assessment](images/fig08_percutaneous_rvad_assessment.jpg)')
    doc.append('*Figure 8: Percutaneous RVAD assessment. (A) ME 4-chamber view with color Doppler showing inflow cannula residing in the right atrium. (B) RVOT view showing outflow cannula crossing the pulmonic valve into the main pulmonary artery with laminar forward flow.*')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('---')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### 6.3. Total Artificial Heart (TAH) & Biventricular Assist Devices (BiVAD)')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('Total Artificial Heart (SynCardia TAH) replacement requires complete excision of native ventricles and native valves, leaving the atrial cuffs, ascending aorta, and main pulmonary artery.')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('<span id="fig-09"></span>')
    doc.append('![Figure 9: Pre-BiVAD imaging strategy](images/fig09_pre_bi_vad_imaging_strategy.jpg)')
    doc.append('*Figure 9: Systematic imaging strategy starting from the ME 4-chamber view prior to BiVAD placement. Systematic assessment of biatrial size, intracardiac shunts, and systemic vascular resistance.*')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('<span id="fig-10"></span>')
    doc.append('![Figure 10: Post-BiVAD imaging strategy](images/fig10_post_bi_vad_imaging_strategy.jpg)')
    doc.append('*Figure 10: Systematic imaging strategy starting from the ME 4-chamber view after BiVAD placement. Interrogation of both inflow and outflow cannulas and septal alignment.*')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('<span id="fig-11"></span>')
    doc.append('![Figure 11: Pre-TAH imaging strategy](images/fig11_pre_tah_imaging_strategy.jpg)')
    doc.append('*Figure 11: Systematic imaging strategy starting from the ME 4-chamber view before Total Artificial Heart (TAH) implantation. Focus on atrial dimensions, vena caval patency, pulmonary venous confluence, and great vessel diameters.*')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('<span id="fig-12"></span>')
    doc.append('![Figure 12: Post-TAH imaging strategy](images/fig12_post_tah_imaging_strategy.jpg)')
    doc.append('*Figure 12: Systematic imaging strategy starting from the ME 4-chamber view after TAH implantation. Interrogation of atrial anastomosis lines, inflow tilting-disc valves, mechanical outflow valves, and unobstructed systemic/pulmonary venous filling.*')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('#### Table 14: Key Points for Imaging in Total Artificial Heart (TAH) Placement')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Phase | Critical TEE Interrogation Points |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Pre-TAH** | • Precise sizing of left and right atrial dimensions to ensure fit of mechanical ventricles.<br>• Exclude persistent left SVC (PLSVC) and anomalous pulmonary venous connections. |')
    doc.append('| **Post-TAH** | • Examine atrial cuffs and suture lines for restriction or anastomotic thrombus.<br>• Interrogate mechanical tilting-disc inflow valves (mitral and tricuspid positions) and outflow valves (aortic and pulmonary positions) for normal disk excursion and velocity profiles.<br>• Exclude systemic or pulmonary venous obstruction. |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('---')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### 6.4. ECMO and Intra-Aortic Balloon Pump (IABP)')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('#### Table 15: Key Points for Extracorporeal Membrane Oxygenation (ECMO)')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| ECMO Configuration | Ultrasound Objectives & Cannula Positioning |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Veno-Arterial (VA) ECMO** | • **Drainage Cannula:** Positioned in mid-to-lower RA or IVC via femoral vein; confirm no contact with interatrial septum.<br>• **Arterial Return:** Guide femoral or axillary return.<br>• **LV Distension Monitoring:** High afterload from VA ECMO may prevent aortic valve opening, leading to acute LV distension, pulmonary edema, and intracardiac stasis/thrombosis. TEE monitors LV dimensions, spontaneous echo contrast, and aortic valve opening. If LV is locked, TEE guides emergent LV venting (Impella, atrial septostomy, transapical vent). |')
    doc.append('| **Veno-Venous (VV) ECMO** | • **Femoral-Jugular:** Drainage in IVC-RA junction, reinfusion in mid-RA directed across tricuspid valve.<br>• **Dual-Lumen Bicaval (Avalon):** Inflow ports in SVC and IVC; reinfusion port positioned strictly in mid-RA aimed directly across the tricuspid valve orifice (verified by CFD in ME bicaval view). |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('#### Table 16: Key Points for Intra-Aortic Balloon Pump (IABP) Placement')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Parameter | TEE Verification Checklist |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Balloon Tip Position** | • Tip must reside **$1	ext{--}2\text{ cm}$ distal to the origin of the left subclavian artery (LSCA)** in the proximal descending thoracic aorta (ME descending aorta view).<br>• Exclude placement too proximal (occlusion of LSCA with left arm ischemia) or too distal (mesenteric/renal malperfusion). |')
    doc.append('| **Complications** | • Real-time screening for aortic dissection, intramural hematoma, and balloon rupture/perforation. |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('</div></details>')
    doc.append('')

    # Section 7: Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy (HCM)
    doc.append('<details class="med-details"><summary>')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('## 7. Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy & Septal Myectomy</summary><div class="details-content">')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### Table 17: Key Points for Preprocedure Imaging in Patients with HCM')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Anatomical Target | TEE Measurement & Assessment |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Interventricular Septum** | • Measure maximal septal thickness at the point of systolic contact with the anterior mitral leaflet in ME LAX and TG mid-papillary SAX views.<br>• Map the longitudinal extent of hypertrophy to plan myectomy trough length and depth. |')
    doc.append('| **Mitral Valve Apparatus** | • Elongated anterior leaflet, anteriorly displaced papillary muscles, bifid or fused papillary muscles, direct insertion of papillary muscle into anterior leaflet, and accessory chordae contributing to dynamic obstruction. |')
    doc.append('| **LVOT Obstruction & SAM** | • Continuous-wave Doppler interrogation of dynamic late-peaking "dagger-shaped" systolic LVOT gradient at rest and with provocation (Valsalva, premature ventricular contraction).<br>• Differentiate dynamic LVOT jet from eccentric mitral regurgitation jet (MR peak velocity $5	ext{--}6\text{ m/s}$, pansystolic). |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('<span id="fig-13"></span>')
    doc.append('![Figure 13: Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with SAM and post-myectomy assessment](images/fig13_hcm_sam_septal_reduction.jpg)')
    doc.append('*Figure 13: Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with SAM. (A) ME long-axis view showing severe asymmetric septal hypertrophy and systolic anterior motion (SAM) of the anterior mitral leaflet causing dynamic LVOT obstruction. (B) ME view post-septal myectomy showing wide-open LVOT trough, absence of SAM, and normalized laminar forward flow.*')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### Table 18: Key Points for Postprocedure Imaging in Patients with HCM')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Assessment Focus | Checklist & Complication Screening |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Myectomy Adequacy** | • Confirm wide, unhindered LVOT lumen; verify complete resolution of SAM.<br>• Residual resting LVOT gradient must be $< 10	ext{--}20\text{ mmHg}$ by CWD (provoked $< 30\text{ mmHg}$). |')
    doc.append('| **Ventricular Septal Defect (VSD)**| • High risk of iatrogenic muscular VSD if myectomy is carried too deep. Interrogate the entire resection trough with high-gain color flow Doppler.<br>• Residual septal thickness should remain $\ge 8	ext{--}10\text{ mm}$. |')
    doc.append('| **Aortic & Mitral Regurgitation** | • Exclude iatrogenic aortic regurgitation (direct surgical trauma to the right coronary cusp during myectomy).<br>• Mitral regurgitation secondary to SAM should completely resolve or reduce to trace/mild; persistent severe MR suggests intrinsic leaflet pathology requiring repair. |')
    doc.append('| **Conduction Injury** | • Left bundle branch block is common; complete heart block occurs in $2	ext{--}5\%$ of patients. |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('</div></details>')
    doc.append('')

    # Section 8: Heart Transplantation
    doc.append('<details class="med-details"><summary>')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('## 8. Orthotopic Heart Transplantation (OHT)</summary><div class="details-content">')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### Table 19: Key Points for Pre- and Postprocedure Assessment in Heart Transplantation')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Phase | Critical TEE Interrogation Points |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Pre-Implantation (Recipient)**| • Exclude pre-existing anatomical variants: persistent left SVC (PLSVC), pulmonary venous anomalies, aortic arch atheroma.<br>• Measure pulmonary artery systolic pressure (PVR assessment) to anticipate donor RV failure. |')
    doc.append('| **Post-Implantation (Donor Heart)**| • **De-Airing:** Meticulous de-airing of biatrial, biventricular, and aortic graft lumens.<br>• **Donor Biventricular Function:** Monitor for acute donor RV failure (secondary to pre-existing recipient pulmonary hypertension and donor organ ischemia); evaluate LV recovery from brain-death catecholamine surge.<br>• **Interatrial Septum:** Rule out patent foramen ovale in the donor heart (color Doppler and agitated saline contrast).<br>• **Systemic Venous Anastomoses:** In bicaval technique, interrogate SVC and IVC anastomoses by CFD and PWD (peak gradient $< 2\text{ mmHg}$); rule out caval stenosis.<br>• **Pulmonary Venous Confluence:** Interrogate left and right pulmonary venous inflows into the donor left atrial cuff for stenosis or kinking. |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('<span id="fig-14"></span>')
    doc.append('![Figure 14: Heart transplantation bicaval anastomosis](images/fig14_heart_transplant_bicaval_anastomosis.jpg)')
    doc.append('*Figure 14: Heart transplantation bicaval anastomosis. (A) ME bicaval view showing laminar flow by color flow Doppler across the superior vena cava (SVC)-right atrial anastomosis without narrowing. (B) Pulsed-wave Doppler demonstrating normal low-velocity phasic systemic venous flow ($< 1.0\text{ m/s}$).*')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('</div></details>')
    doc.append('')

    # Section 9: Lung Transplantation
    doc.append('<details class="med-details"><summary>')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('## 9. Lung Transplantation</summary><div class="details-content">')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### Table 20: Key Points for Imaging in Patients Undergoing Lung Transplantation')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Stage | Echocardiographic Objectives & Vascular Anastomoses |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Pre-Transplant** | • Quantify pulmonary artery systolic pressure and baseline RV function.<br>• Screen for intracardiac shunts (PFO/ASD); acute elevation in right atrial pressure during single-lung ventilation can trigger severe hypoxemic right-to-left shunting. |')
    doc.append('| **Post-Transplant** | • **Pulmonary Artery Anastomosis:** Interrogate donor-recipient pulmonary artery anastomoses in UE and high ME views. Significant stenosis ($V_{max} > 2.5\text{ m/s}$ or mean gradient $> 10\text{ mmHg}$) indicates kinking or narrowing requiring immediate surgical revision.<br>• **Pulmonary Venous Anastomosis:** Interrogate right and left pulmonary venous cuff anastomoses. Velocity $> 1.0	ext{--}1.5\text{ m/s}$ with loss of normal biphasic flow suggests anastomotic narrowing or torsion, predisposing to acute lung graft congestion.<br>• **RV Remodeling:** Monitor acute RV decompression after lung implantation. |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('<span id="fig-15"></span>')
    doc.append('![Figure 15: Lung transplantation pulmonary vein anastomosis](images/fig15_lung_transplant_pv_anastomosis.jpg)')
    doc.append('*Figure 15: Lung transplantation pulmonary vein anastomosis. (A) ME right upper pulmonary vein (RUPV) view demonstrating narrowing at the donor-recipient atrial cuff anastomosis. (B) Spectral Doppler interrogation demonstrating continuous turbulent high-velocity flow indicative of anastomotic obstruction.*')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('</div></details>')
    doc.append('')

    # Section 10: Pericardial Diseases
    doc.append('<details class="med-details"><summary>')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('## 10. Pericardial Diseases</summary><div class="details-content">')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### Table 21: Key Points for Intraoperative Imaging in Patients with Pericardial Diseases')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Clinical Entity | Pathophysiology & TEE Diagnostic Signs |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Cardiac Tamponade** | • **Chamber Collapse:** Right atrial diastolic collapse (lasting $> 1/3$ of cardiac cycle; sensitive early sign); right ventricular free wall early diastolic collapse (highly specific).<br>• **IVC Plethora:** Fixed, dilated IVC without respiratory variation.<br>• **Respiratory Flow Variations:** Exaggerated respiratory variation in mitral inflow ($E\text{ velocity} > 25	ext{--}30\%$) and tricuspid inflow ($> 40	ext{--}50\%$).<br>• **Guidance:** Real-time needle guidance during subxiphoid or percutaneous drainage; confirmation of complete evacuation. |')
    doc.append('| **Constrictive Pericarditis** | • **Pericardial Thickening / Calcification:** Hyperechoic, immobile pericardial reflection on transgastric views.<br>• **Ventricular Interdependence:** Exaggerated respiratory septal bounce ("shiver" or leftward shift on inspiration).<br>• **Hepatic Vein Doppler:** Exaggerated diastolic expiratory flow reversal.<br>• **Mitral Annulus Resonances:** *Annulus paradoxus* (medial $e\' \ge 9\text{ cm/s}$ despite heart failure) and *annulus reversus* (medial $e\' >$ lateral $e\'$).<br>• **Post-Pericardiectomy:** Assess immediate improvement in diastolic filling and monitor for acute biventricular dilation. |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('</div></details>')
    doc.append('')

    # Section 11: Tumors and Intracardiac Masses
    doc.append('<details class="med-details"><summary>')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('## 11. Tumors and Intracardiac Masses</summary><div class="details-content">')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('### Table 22: Key Points for Intraoperative Imaging in Resection of Intracardiac Masses')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('| Phase | TEE Checklist Items |')
    doc.append('| :--- | :--- |')
    doc.append('| **Pre-Resection** | • Determine exact site of stalk attachment (e.g., fossa ovalis for myxomas, valve leaflet for papillary fibroelastomas, atrial wall for sarcomas).<br>• Characterize mobility, fragmentation risk, and obstruction of valve orifices or venous inflows.<br>• Differentiate tumor from thrombus (contrast enhancement: vascularized tumors enhance; avascular thrombi do not). |')
    doc.append('| **Post-Resection** | • Confirm complete surgical excision with clear margins.<br>• Exclude iatrogenic atrial septal defect or valve injury created during wide margin excision.<br>• Thorough de-airing and screening for embolized mass fragments in ventricular apex or LVOT. |')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('</div></details>')
    doc.append('')

    # Section 12: Summary and Conclusions
    doc.append('<details class="med-details"><summary>')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('## 12. Summary and Conclusions</summary><div class="details-content">')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('Transesophageal echocardiography has become an indispensable extension of the cardiovascular surgical team. Adopting a structured, surgery-based protocol guarantees standardized, reproducible, and rigorous assessment across all phases of the operative procedure.')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('By executing a systematic pre-CPB scan, communicating dynamic findings to surgeons, optimizing cardiopulmonary bypass weaning, immediately verifying repair adequacy, and proactively intercepting iatrogenic mechanical complications, perioperative echocardiographers directly safeguard patient outcomes across the entire spectrum of modern cardiovascular surgery.')
    doc.append('')
    doc.append('</div></details>')
    doc.append('')

